# Who must approve what.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FILE is separation of duties. Three DIFFERENT questions need three DIFFERENT
# approvers, and collapsing any two of them makes the control self-referential:
#
#   pipeline-maintainers   "What does the control do?"        -- owns the machinery
#   security-approvers     "May this finding pass?"           -- owns exceptions ONLY
#   deploy-approvers       "May this change reach prod?"      -- owns promotion
#
# WHY THEY CANNOT BE THE SAME TEAM
#
# An earlier version of this file gave security-approvers both `/dispositions/` AND
# `reusable-security.yml`. That is self-referential authority: if you can edit the gate, you never
# need a disposition -- you simply make it pass. The exception process becomes theatre, because the
# people governing it can redefine what "secure" means instead of requesting an exception to it.
#
# Likewise deploy approval must NOT implicitly grant a security exception. "This change may reach
# prod" and "this finding may pass" are different claims with different evidence.
#
# ROLES, NEVER PEOPLE. A pipeline routed to an individual becomes an undocumented API and needs
# reconstructive surgery when that person moves on. Populate the team; never hardcode a name. One
# person may occupy several roles in a small estate -- but that collapse must be DELIBERATE and
# visible here, not an accident of who happened to have permissions.
#
# ⚠️ THIS FILE ENFORCES NOTHING ON ITS OWN. CODEOWNERS *requests* a reviewer. Branch protection must
# REQUIRE review from code owners, or this is documentation pretending to be a control -- the same
# declared-versus-enforced gap this kit exists to eliminate. Verify it, do not assume it.

# ── THE PROTECTED OWNER (KE-05, KDEC-050, 2026-11-11) ────────────────────────────
# Every path that changes what the control DOES -- the floor, the scanner registry, the policy
# register, the workflows and composites, the validators (scripts/, tests/), the schemas, templates,
# profiles, the API, and this file itself -- has ONE owner: the team named on the line below.
# ONE, because GitHub accepts approval from ANY ONE owner listed on the matching line: a line
# reading `@a @b` lets either team approve alone, so "both teams sign" was never enforced by this
# file. If two teams must both sign, put the people who may approve control changes in this one
# team and require two approvals in the ruleset (KDEC-050 mitigation, PROPOSED).
#
# The placeholder team name says what to do. scripts/protected_paths.py --check-codeowners (in the
# self-test contract job) holds every protected path to this owner, and REFUSES the placeholder in an
# adopted copy (one carrying the export watermark): a placeholder team owns nothing. Replace it here
# AND on the declaration line.
# kit-protected-owner: @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners

# ── The machinery: what the control DOES ────────────────────────────────────────
# Changing these changes what "secure" means for every consumer. Formerly
# @YOUR_ORG/pipeline-maintainers: populate the protected owner with them.
/.github/                    @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners
/assurance-taxonomy.yaml     @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners
/scripts/                    @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners
/tests/                      @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners
/api/                        @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners
/templates/                  @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners
/schemas/                    @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners
/rollback-resource-classes.yaml  @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners
/CODEOWNERS                  @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners
/docs/CODEOWNERS             @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners

# ── Policy: how hard it enforces ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Profiles decide adoption-vs-strict and which capabilities are required; the central policy
# register decides what every repository inherits. Security decisions about enforcement posture:
# the protected owner's membership must include the security approvers' view (see above).
/profiles/                   @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners
/policy/                     @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners

# ── The protected capability floor and the scanner registry (KDEC-033, KDEC-050) ──
# What the gate must expect WHATEVER scanner-registry.yaml says. Kit-owned, and it only shrinks
# through a tombstone with a recorded decision. The registry is the population denominator: a row
# removed there is a scanner removed. Their checkers and the composite are under /scripts/ and
# /.github/ above.
/capability-floor.json       @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners
/scanner-registry.yaml       @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners
# The scanner obligations (KDEC-069): what discovery makes a repository owe, who may provide it, provider
# retirements, gap acceptances and control retirements. A control is removed here only by a recorded decision.
/scanner-obligations.json    @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners
# Mappings a person made for ids the kit could not read (owner ruling 2026-11-06, scripts/unknown_ids.py):
# a mapping changes which family a finding joins.
/id-mappings.json            @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners
# The governance profile (KDEC-059): which authorizer a protected change needs. The gate reads it from
# the BASE only, and a change to it is itself protected.
/governance-profile.json     @YOUR_ORG/replace-me-kit-control-owners

# ── Exceptions: may THIS finding pass ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Security approves exceptions and NOTHING ELSE. Deliberately no ownership of the gate itself.
/dispositions/                             @YOUR_ORG/security-approvers
/schemas/disposition.schema.json           @YOUR_ORG/security-approvers
/schemas/disposition-request.schema.json   @YOUR_ORG/security-approvers

# Requests are NOT owned by security -- anyone may open one. They are reviewed, not gate-kept at
# authorship. Gate-keeping the request instead of the approval just stops people asking, and a team
# that cannot ask for a review disables the workflow instead.

# ── Promotion: may this change reach an environment ─────────────────────────────
# Environment protection rules are configured in repository settings, not here. deploy-approvers is
# named as the required reviewer THERE. Listed in this comment so the third role is visible in the
# same place as the other two rather than living only in a settings page nobody reads.
#   @YOUR_ORG/deploy-approvers  -> required reviewers on the `prod` environment
